---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import adventuresImg from '../../assets/20240628_150045.jpg';
import mountainImg   from '../../assets/20240815_154043.jpg';
import hikingImg     from '../../assets/20240510_112320.jpg';
import cyclingImg    from '../../assets/20240406_145029.jpg';
import packraftImg   from '../../assets/20250302_135817~2.jpg';
import climbImg   from '../../assets/20250302_135817~2.jpg';
---

<PageLayout
	title="Outdoors — Thomas R. Colin"
	description="Mountains, expeditions, and wild places. Climbing, mountaineering, hiking, cycling, packrafting, and sailing."
	bodyClass="theme-outdoors"
>
	<div class="page-header">
		<h1>Outdoors</h1>
		<p class="lead">
			Being outside — in the mountains, on rivers, on a bike, or on foot — is a central part of
			my life. Here's an overview of what I do and where I've been.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="content">

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Adventures</h2>
				<p>
					Some trips stand apart and deserve their own pages. Some of these were "extreme" ... to me. What 
					makes something a true adventure is not its inherent difficulty, but the way it pushes at my limits
					and engages me wholly. My trips to Patagonia, Norway, and the Bolivian Amazon were personal challenges
					that transformed me. True adventures, isolated in remote wildernesses, where decision-making is critical.

					Other trips, like the Dent du Géant, the Atlantic crossing, or even my short climbing holiday in the
					Calanques, were remarkable in other ways: social challenges, otherworldly natural beauty...
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="/adventures/patagonia-2024">Patagonia 2024</a></li>
					<li>Dent du Géant 2024 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Écrins 2024 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Norway 2025 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Atlantic crossing 2025 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Patagonia 2025 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Bolivian Amazon 2025 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
					<li>Calanques 2026 <span class="soon">(coming soon)</span></li>
				</ul>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={adventuresImg} alt="Adventures" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Mountaineering</h2>
				<p>
					I really got into climbing mountains around 2022. Since then I've "bagged" a few summits.
					But it's not about making it to the top! It's about having a good time with my friends, 
					making memories, meeting risks but staying safe, moving with purpose, body
					and mind fully engaged. It's all rather useless, but it's as beautiful as life gets.
				</p>
				<p>
					I keep a full log of routes and certifications/training on the
					<a href="/outdoors/climbing">mountaineering page</a>.
				</p>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={mountainImg} alt="Mountaineering" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Climbing</h2>
				<p>
					I've been climbing rocks my entire life. I'm not that good at it: I'm still trying to
					get into the 7 range (I think I can redpoint 7a outdoors but I haven't found the right project,
					and the sandbagged Belgium cliffs aren't helping).
					Doesn't change how much I enjoy it! Instead of getting good, I've been learning every climbing-related
					discipline: bouldering, lead, multipitch, trad, ice climbing...
				</p>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={climbImg} alt="climbing" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Hiking and running</h2>
				<p>
					It's essential prep for expeditions! I run or hike 2-3 times a week.
				</p>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={hikingImg} alt="Hiking" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Cycling</h2>
				<p>
					I've bikepacked in Quebec, Cornwall, around the Alps
					and Pyrénées, in Chilean Patagonia and in Chiloe.
				</p>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={cyclingImg} alt="Cycling" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<div class="section-row">
			<section class="section-text">
				<h2>Packrafting</h2>
				<p>
					Packrafting combines hiking and light inflatable rafting. A packraft weighs roughly
					2–4 kg and packs down small enough to carry in a very large backpack, alongside a dry suit for cold water,
					a personal floation device (PFD or life jacket), a helmet, paddle, and a bunch of accessories.
					In the end it's a lot of stuff to lug around. But it makes it possible to cross
					rivers, lakes, and fjords that would otherwise be impassable, enabling access to some of the most isolated
					regions on earth. And for as long as you're gliding on the water, all that stuff is weightless. 
				</p>
				<p>
					I've used packrafts in Patagonia, Norway, and in the Amazon rainforest; and in many smaller trips in
					France and Belgium.
				</p>
			</section>
			<div class="section-photo">
				<Image src={packraftImg} alt="Packrafting" width={300} height={400} />
			</div>
		</div>

		<hr />

		<section class="section-text solo-section">
			<h2>Sailing</h2>
			<p>
				I haven't done much sailing: I used to go on those tiny "optimiste" boats on holidays as a child. 
				For my 2025 trip in Latin America I wanted to avoid taking the plane for climate reasons, so I joined
				the "trainees"/passengers of a tall ship, the Morgenster. It was challenging! I think mountaineering is
				more my thing, but this was still a beautiful adventure and I hope I'll get back to sailing on smaller
				ships eventually. 
				[link to atlantic crossing adventure page]
			</p>
		</section>

	</div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	.page-header {
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		margin-bottom: 2.5em;
		padding-bottom: 1em;
	}
	.page-header h1 {
		font-size: 2.2em;
		margin: 0 0 0.4em;
	}
	.lead {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 1em;
		margin: 0;
		max-width: 600px;
	}

	.section-row {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 240px;
		gap: 2.5em;
		align-items: start;
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	.section-text h2 {
		font-size: 1.4em;
		margin-bottom: 0.6em;
	}
	.section-text p {
		font-size: 0.95em;
		line-height: 1.75;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
	}
	.section-text ul {
		padding-left: 1.25em;
		margin: 0.25em 0 0;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.35em;
	}
	.section-text li {
		font-size: 0.95em;
	}
	.soon {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 0.85em;
	}
	.section-photo img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 320px;
		object-fit: cover;
		border-radius: 6px;
		display: block;
	}
	.solo-section {
		max-width: 620px;
	}
	hr {
		margin: 2em 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 680px) {
		.section-row {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
		.section-photo {
			order: -1;
		}
		.section-photo img {
			height: 220px;
		}
	}
</style>
